data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

The EF Core Cosmos DB provider has been on a path to **fully remove synchronous query support**. In .NET 10, you could opt into sync-over-async behavior (e.g., via `EnableSyncOverAsync()`), but in .NET 11 **that opt-in is removed entirely**.

All four of your methods will throw `InvalidOperationException` at runtime:

| Method | Sync call that breaks | Async replacement |
|---|---|---|
| `GetAll()` | `ToList()` | `await ToListAsync()` |
| `GetById()` | `First()` | `await FirstAsync()` |
| `GetCount()` | `Count()` | `await CountAsync()` |
| `Save()` | `SaveChanges()` | `await SaveChangesAsync()` |

### The fix — convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- The async LINQ methods live in `Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore` (add `using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore;`).
- This will ripple up your call chain — callers must also become `async Task`/`async Task<T>`.
- `Add()` stays synchronous (it's an in-memory tracking call, not a database operation). Only the query/save calls need conversion.
- Remove any `EnableSyncOverAsync()` configuration — it will cause a compile error in .NET 11.
